    {
      "family": "iterative",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 14, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0,
      "why": "iterative is the smallest-area family (one shared datapath, no per-stage registers). It is the only family that can plausibly minimise area, but it emits one result every N+3 cycles, so it can only meet >=1 MSPS at low Fmax. I keep n_iter at 10-14 so that at a realistic Fmax (tens of MHz) throughput stays >=1 MSPS, and data_width 12-16 with modest guards to hold max_abs_err <= 2^-10 while keeping area small. This maps the low-area corner of the front."
    },
    {
      "family": "unrolled_k",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 12, "high": 16, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 10, "high": 14, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "k", "low": 2, "high": 4, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 1,
      "why": "unrolled_k chains k micro-rotations per cycle, so it reaches >=1 MSPS at a much lower Fmax than iterative for the same N, and it is the natural way to satisfy the throughput constraint while staying close to the iterative area (shared datapath, only a few extra shifters/registers). k=2-4 keeps the combinational depth (and hence Fmax) manageable. I focus the budget here because it is where the feasible, low-area, throughput-meeting designs live; the trade-off between k (area/Fmax) and n_iter (accuracy) is the interesting curve to map."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "No fleet facts yet, so I start from first principles on the two families I own. The spec is area-minimisation with two hard constraints: throughput >= 1 MSPS and max_abs_err <= 2^-10. Accuracy depends only on data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard, rounding (identical bits across families), so I bound those to the smallest values that can plausibly clear 2^-10 (data_width 12-16, n_iter 10-14, small guards, both rounding modes) and let the bit-accurate model confirm feasibility. The real differentiator between my two families is throughput: iterative is the cheapest in area but emits one result every N+3 cycles, so it only clears 1 MSPS at low Fmax; unrolled_k divides the cycle count by k and is the robust way to meet throughput while staying near the iterative area. I therefore put the full budget on unrolled_k (k=2-4) to map the feasible low-area/throughput-meeting region, and keep a zero-share iterative entry only to record the absolute low-area reference point if it happens to meet throughput. This concentrates evaluations where the trade-off (k vs n_iter vs data_width) actually lives, rather than sweeping the full registry ranges."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "The Pareto front is dominated by a single feasible design from the `iterative` family: [data_width=16, n_iter=14, angle_guard=2, frac_guard=2, rounding=trunc] with an area of 7860 um^2 and accuracy of 11.9 bits. This design satisfies both constraints (throughput 7.7 MSPS >= 1, max_abs_err 0.00027 <= 0.000976562). The `unrolled_k` family, which was heavily explored (55 evals), yielded a best feasible area of 9189 um^2, which is significantly larger than the `iterative` solution. Since the goal is to minimize area and the `iterative` design is the global minimum found so far, and we are at the final round with no budget left for further exploration or front mapping, stopping is the correct action. The hypervolume is 5.954e+04, which is reasonable given the single-point front, but no further improvement is possible within the constraints and budget."
  ,
  "next_plan": null
}
